+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

XERJ is a local search engine and MCP server.  The package installs the
`xerj` binary and an example configuration at:

    ${LOCALBASE}/share/examples/xerj/xerj.toml

XERJ has no default configuration search path.  Start a server with an
explicit configuration file:

    xerj --config /path/to/xerj.toml

Without `--config`, compiled defaults are used and `./data` is created
in the current directory.  Keep that directory outside any folder you
later index, or XERJ will index its own storage.

Authentication and TLS
----------------------

Authentication is enabled by default and TLS is disabled by default.
The first start creates an admin key at `<data_dir>/admin.key` with mode
0600. Every client must send that key, including `xerj autoindex`.  Do
not use `--insecure`: it disables both authentication and TLS.  Keep the
default loopback bind unless TLS or a TLS-terminating proxy protects
every listener; a cleartext non-loopback bind requires an explicit
opt-in.

The gRPC listener on port 8081 is cleartext h2c even when the REST
listeners use TLS.  Do not expose port 8081 outside a trusted host or a
TLS-terminating proxy.  A self-signed certificate is generated only when
TLS is enabled without certificate paths; supply a real certificate for
any non-local service.

The autoindex and corpus clients validate HTTPS certificates before sending
index data or API keys. Self-signed servers require their certificate
authority to be trusted by the client; an untrusted certificate is rejected
even on loopback. The port removes upstream's implicit certificate bypass.

Data, models and service
------------------------

The data directory is supplied by the administrator and should be
writable only by the account running XERJ.  It contains the admin key,
session material and index contents, so treat backups as sensitive.

The default `neural` and `decide-local` features are built in, but the
default embedding mode is lexical and nothing is downloaded at startup.
A neural model (about 90 MB) is fetched only when `--embed-mode neural`
is selected or configured.  Pre-download it or use a local model
directory when the service has no outbound network.

OpenBSD has no RLIMIT_AS, so isolated PDF and spreadsheet workers cannot
apply their upstream address-space cap. The spreadsheet CPU limit and
worker process-group cleanup remain; PDF workers retain their timeout.
This is not a memory sandbox. Automatic total/available RAM and RSS probes
are also unavailable on OpenBSD. Set memory and worker budgets explicitly
rather than assuming that a memory-derived limit is enforced.

The rc.101 source tag still declares rc.100 in its Cargo workspace, so
--version reports rc.100. The package version identifies the rc.101 tag;
the port preserves upstream's source metadata.

No rc.d service or dedicated user is installed in this initial port.
Runtime permissions, HOME-dependent pointer files, signal handling and
pledge/unveil behaviour still need validation on OpenBSD.

The standard OpenBSD PDF and legacy XLS parser workers close inherited
non-protocol descriptors, unveil only the input file for reading, and
pledge stdio/rpath before parsing it. Worker startup fails if either
restriction cannot be installed. The supervisor clears the environment
for these same-binary workers so server credentials are not inherited.
Neither policy permits execution, networking or file modification.

These restrictions cover the fresh parser workers, not the supervisor,
other extractors, or arbitrary programs selected through the trusted
XERJ_PDF_WORKER_BIN and XERJ_XLS_WORKER_BIN developer overrides. The
supervisor remains responsible for file selection and command execution.
Bounded output, timeouts and CPU limits do not provide an address-space
limit on OpenBSD. Native validation of parser resources, signal handling,
non-UTF-8 paths and symlink access remains required.
